CAT = "arrtctSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
_dlp = 12 * 40; assert _dlp == 480
_ctdivol = 20 / 1.25; assert _ctdivol == 16
_e = 500 * 0.015; assert abs(_e - 7.5) < 1e-9
_isq = 1 / 3**2; assert abs(_isq - 1/9) < 1e-9
Q = [
("A chest CT has a CTDIvol of 12 mGy over a 40 cm scan length. What is the dose-length product?",
 "480 mGy-cm", ["52 mGy-cm", "120 mGy-cm", "4,800 mGy-cm"],
 "DLP equals CTDIvol times scan length: 12 mGy x 40 cm = 480 mGy-cm. Adding the two numbers or moving the decimal gives the wrong answers."),
("A helical scan has a weighted CTDI of 20 mGy and a pitch of 1.25. What is the CTDIvol?",
 "16 mGy", ["25 mGy", "20 mGy", "12.5 mGy"],
 "CTDIvol equals CTDIw divided by pitch: 20 / 1.25 = 16 mGy. Multiplying by pitch gives 25 mGy, the error of thinking a higher pitch adds dose."),
("A scan's DLP is 500 mGy-cm, and the conversion factor for the region is 0.015 mSv per mGy-cm. What is the estimated effective dose?",
 "7.5 mSv", ["0.75 mSv", "33 mSv", "75 mSv"],
 "Effective dose is estimated as DLP times the region's k factor: 500 x 0.015 = 7.5 mSv. The factor depends on body region and patient age."),
("Which dose index displayed on the CT console describes the average dose within the scanned volume?",
 "CTDIvol", ["DLP", "Effective dose", "Entrance skin dose"],
 "CTDIvol (mGy) is the average dose per slice location in a standard phantom, corrected for pitch. DLP multiplies it by length to describe the whole scan."),
("In which unit is the dose-length product reported?",
 "mGy-cm", ["mSv", "mGy", "Gy per minute"],
 "DLP is CTDIvol (mGy) times scan length (cm), so its unit is mGy-cm. Effective dose is in millisieverts."),
("Holding all other factors constant, raising pitch from 1.0 to 1.5 has what effect on patient dose?",
 "Dose decreases", ["Dose increases", "Dose is unchanged", "Dose doubles"],
 "A higher pitch moves the table farther per rotation, so each part of the body spends less time in the beam and dose falls, unless the scanner raises mA to compensate."),
("Halving the mAs while keeping kVp and pitch the same changes the patient dose how?",
 "Halves it", ["Doubles it", "Quarters it", "Leaves it unchanged"],
 "Dose is directly proportional to mAs. Halving mAs halves dose and raises image noise by about the square root of two."),
("Which change raises the mean energy of the CT beam and makes it more penetrating?",
 "Higher kVp", ["Higher mA", "Longer rotation time", "Wider display window"],
 "kVp sets photon energy and beam quality. mA and rotation time change the number of photons, not their energy."),
("What is the main purpose of the shaped bowtie filter in a CT gantry?",
 "Even out dose across the patient", ["Remove scatter from the detectors", "Narrow the beam along the z-axis", "Raise the beam's peak energy"],
 "The bowtie filter attenuates the beam more at the edges, where the patient is thinner, evening out detector signal and cutting surface dose. Post-patient collimators reduce scatter."),
("A patient is centered 5 cm below the gantry isocenter. What happens with automatic tube current modulation?",
 "Dose and noise may both rise", ["Dose always falls", "Image noise is unaffected", "The bowtie filter compensates fully"],
 "Off-center positioning changes the scout's apparent size and moves the body away from the bowtie filter's design, which can raise surface dose and noise. Centering at isocenter is part of dose control."),
("What is over-ranging in helical CT?",
 "Exposure beyond the planned range", ["Scanning a wider field of view", "Repeating a scan for motion", "Using a pitch above 2.0"],
 "Helical interpolation needs data from beyond each end of the planned range, so extra rotations are exposed. Adaptive collimators block part of that extra beam."),
("Compared with retrospective gating, prospective ECG triggering for coronary CTA usually:",
 "Lowers patient dose", ["Raises patient dose", "Scans every cardiac phase", "Needs a lower pitch"],
 "Prospective triggering turns the beam on only in a chosen phase, often mid-diastole, so dose is much lower. Retrospective gating exposes throughout the cycle."),
("Replacing filtered backprojection with iterative reconstruction allows which change at the same image noise?",
 "Lower tube current", ["Higher tube current", "Thinner bowtie filter", "Larger focal spot"],
 "Iterative reconstruction models noise and suppresses it, so a similar-looking image can be made from fewer photons. That is why it is a dose-reduction tool."),
("Angular tube current modulation adjusts the mA mainly according to:",
 "Body thickness at each angle", ["Heart rate during the scan", "Contrast arrival in the aorta", "Detector temperature"],
 "Angular modulation lowers mA through thin projections, such as front to back in the chest, and raises it laterally where the body is wider. Longitudinal modulation varies it along the z-axis."),
("Before one series, the console says its planned CTDIvol exceeds the protocol's preset level for that series. What is this called?",
 "Dose notification", ["Dose summary report", "Scout warning", "Exposure log"],
 "A notification appears before scanning when a planned value passes the protocol's preset level, so the technologist can confirm. A dose alert is a higher, cumulative level that needs a reason to proceed."),
("A scan is accidentally repeated at the wrong protocol, giving a much higher dose than planned. What should the technologist do?",
 "Report it as an adverse event", ["Delete the extra series", "Note it only on the requisition", "Wait to see if symptoms appear"],
 "Scanning errors that cause unplanned dose are reported through the facility's adverse event process, and suspected overexposures may also be reported to FDA MedWatch. Deleting images hides the record."),
("Which step most reduces dose for a child having a head CT?",
 "Child-sized kVp and mAs", ["Adult head protocol", "Wider scan range", "Lower pitch, same mAs"],
 "Children are more radiosensitive, and adult settings give more dose than needed. Protocols sized to the child, as Image Gently promotes, are the main step."),
("Breathing instructions before a chest CT help reduce dose mainly by:",
 "Fewer repeats for motion", ["Lowering the kVp needed", "Shortening the gantry rotation", "Filtering low-energy photons"],
 "A patient who understands the breath-hold is less likely to move, and motion is a common cause of repeated scans and extra dose."),
("At diagnostic CT energies in soft tissue, which photon interaction predominates?",
 "Compton scattering", ["Coherent scattering", "Pair production", "Photodisintegration"],
 "At typical CT effective energies (about 60 to 70 keV) Compton scattering dominates in soft tissue, with photoelectric absorption mattering more in bone and iodine. Pair production needs over 1.02 MeV."),
("Iodine is a strong CT contrast agent mainly because its K-edge favors which interaction?",
 "Photoelectric absorption", ["Compton scattering", "Coherent scattering", "Characteristic emission"],
 "Iodine's high atomic number and K-edge at about 33 keV make photoelectric absorption likely, so iodinated vessels look bright. Lower kVp moves the beam closer to that edge."),
("A technologist stands 1 m from a patient during a CT-guided procedure and then steps back to 3 m. How does the scatter exposure change?",
 "One-ninth as much", ["One-third as much", "One-sixth as much", "Nine times more"],
 "By the inverse square law, tripling distance cuts intensity to 1/3 squared, which is 1/9. Distance is one of the simplest protections."),
("What protects staff from entering the CT room while the beam is on?",
 "Controlled access and beam-on lights", ["Lead drape on the gantry", "Bowtie filter in the gantry", "Post-patient collimation"],
 "Controlled access, door signs and beam-on lights keep staff and visitors out during exposure. Filters and collimators shape the beam but do not keep people out."),
("A parent must stay beside a young child during a head CT. What should the parent wear?",
 "Lead apron", ["Dosimeter only", "Surgical mask and gloves", "Nothing at CT energies"],
 "Anyone who must stay in the room during exposure wears a lead apron and stands out of the primary beam. Scatter at CT energies is real, so the apron is needed."),
("A pregnant patient needs an abdominal CT for suspected appendicitis. Which approach fits?",
 "Limit range, keep dose low", ["Cancel the scan without review", "Double the mAs for clarity", "Scan two contrast phases"],
 "When CT is justified in pregnancy, the scan is limited to the needed range and phases with the lowest dose that answers the question. Extra phases add dose for little gain."),
]
